{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"files","title":"Reading & Writing Files","group":"Working with Files","order":1,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Most data science starts with a file. Python's built-in <code>open()</code> reads and writes text files — and a few string methods already give you basic text statistics.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>File object</strong> — what <code>open(path, mode)</code> returns: a handle with <code>read</code>, <code>readline</code>, <code>readlines</code>, <code>write</code> and <code>seek</code>. Always close it — or better, use <code>with</code>, which closes it for you.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Modes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Meaning</th><th>If the file exists</th><th>If it doesn't</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>'r'</code></td><td>Read (default)</td><td>Reads from the start</td><td><code>FileNotFoundError</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'w'</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>Erases</strong> it first</td><td>Creates it</td></tr><tr><td><code>'a'</code></td><td>Append</td><td>Writes at the end</td><td>Creates it</td></tr><tr><td><code>'r+'</code></td><td>Read and write</td><td>Starts at the beginning</td><td><code>FileNotFoundError</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'x'</code></td><td>Create only</td><td><code>FileExistsError</code></td><td>Creates it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Writing: write vs writelines</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>lines = [\"This is Delhi \\n\", \"This is Paris \\n\", \"This is London \\n\"]\nwith open(\"myfile.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Hello \\n\")          # one string\n    f.writelines(lines)          # an iterable of strings (no newlines are added for you)\n\nwith open(\"myfile.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hello\nThis is Delhi\nThis is Paris\nThis is London</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-book\"></i>Reading: read, readline, readlines, seek</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>with open(\"myfile.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(repr(f.read(9)))       # 'Hello \\nTh'   ← 9 characters, across lines\n    f.seek(0)                    # jump back to the start\n    print(repr(f.readline()))    # 'Hello \\n'     ← up to and including the newline\n    f.seek(0)\n    print(f.readlines())         # ['Hello \\n', 'This is Delhi \\n', 'This is Paris \\n', 'This is London \\n']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>f.read()</code> / <code>f.read(n)</code></td><td>The whole file / the next <code>n</code> characters, as one string</td></tr><tr><td><code>f.readline()</code></td><td>The next line, including its <code>\\n</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>f.readlines()</code></td><td>A list of all remaining lines</td></tr><tr><td><code>for line in f:</code></td><td>Lines one at a time — memory-friendly for big files</td></tr><tr><td><code>f.seek(n)</code></td><td>Moves the position to byte <code>n</code> (0 = start)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-plus\"></i>Append vs write</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>with open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Today \\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\", \"a\") as f:        # append keeps what was there\n    f.write(\"Tomorrow \\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    print(f.readlines())                  # ['Today \\n', 'Tomorrow \\n']\n\nwith open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") as f:        # write erases it first\n    f.write(\"Only this \\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    print(f.readlines())                  # ['Only this \\n']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Opening with <code>'w'</code> silently wipes the file. Use <code>'a'</code> to add to it, or <code>'x'</code> to refuse overwriting.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Quick text statistics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>text = \"\"\"Data science turns raw data into insight. It mixes statistics and programming.\n\nPython is the most popular language for it.\"\"\"\n\nparagraphs = [p for p in text.split(\"\\n\\n\") if p.strip()]\nsentences = [s for s in text.replace(\"\\n\", \" \").split(\".\") if s.strip()]\nwords = text.split()                     # split() with no argument handles any whitespace\n\nprint(len(paragraphs), len(sentences), len(words))   # 2 3 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>split(\" \")</code> counts empty strings between double spaces and keeps newlines inside words — <code>split()</code> with no argument is almost always what you want for words.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eraser\"></i>Cleaning unwanted characters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>raw = \"Hello!! World@ #2026 [7] [12]\"\nfor ref in [f\"[{i}]\" for i in range(1, 20)]:     # remove citation markers like [7]\n    raw = raw.replace(ref, \"\")\nfor ch in \"!@#\":                                 # remove unwanted symbols\n    raw = raw.replace(ch, \"\")\nprint(\" \".join(raw.split()))                     # Hello World 2026</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>For anything more than a few characters, a regular expression is cleaner: <code>re.sub(r\"\\[\\d+\\]|[!@#]\", \"\", raw)</code> — see <em>Regular Expressions</em>.</p></div></section>"}